WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.060
Well, it's finally happened.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.399
Somehow, Marvel Studios figured out how to
take one of its least interesting movie characters

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:11.019
— the mighty Thor — and make him the star
of a completely bonkers space-adventure with

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.340
more laughs per minute than most recent comedies.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.840
Wasn't Ant-Man supposed to be the funny Marvel
movie?

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.480
How the heck did Thor: Ragnarok become the
funny one?

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.170
"He doesn't know how to get in touch with
you."

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.480
"I don't, I don't have a phone."

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.760
"He doesn't have a phone."

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.140
"Send a raven."

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.960
Anyway, now that the credits have rolled and
the Easter Egg hunts have begun, there's just

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.000
one big question left to answer: what's it
all mean?

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.160
While you probably get the big, overall kinda
stuff — like how the good guys won, and

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.500
the bad guys lost — there's still plenty
of Asgardian goodness we can dive into.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.900
Hold onto your hammers while we explain the
ending of Thor: Ragnarok.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.839
And before you get your breastplate in a twist,
just remember: spoilers for this movie, and

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.960
possibly future Marvel Movies, lie ahead.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.980
Next stop: Oklahoma

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:57.780
So, the realm of Asgard was destroyed by Surtur
in a fury of fire and, hey, maybe some extra

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.280
fury thrown in for good measure.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.280
But as Thor reminds us as he evacuates his
people onto the big honkin' spaceship, Asgard

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.900
isn't a place: it's a people.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:06.600
And those people?

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.180
They're going to Oklahoma.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:08.920
Probably.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.780
Sure, they never mention the the Sooner State
when they decide to hightail it to Midgard

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:13.980
at the end of the movie.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.539
But there's still evidence that Oklahoma is
where they'll make their home in the next

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.920
movie in this series, Thor 4: More Thor Than
Ever Before.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.120
That's because in 2007, the Odinson set up
a new version of Asgard for his displaced

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.940
people in the fictional town of Broxton, Oklahoma.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.460
Is it a guarantee that Thor and his pals will
trade their helmets for cowboy hats?

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.060
Certainly not.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.359
But considering how wacky and crazy Ragnarok
turned out to be, the fish-out-of-water potential

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.520
for setting the next Thor movie in rural,
midwest America is too great to ignore.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.660
Hela's fiery fate

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.380
Near the end of the film, Hela rushes into
battle against the fire demon, Surtur, who

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.420
brings about Ragnarok — the end of Asgard.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.040
That's the last we see of Hela.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.060
And that means, of course, that we never see
her actually die.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.200
That's important!

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.900
When we don't see characters bite the dust,
that means there's plenty of room for them

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.320
to come back in future films.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.720
That's even more true for characters of the
immortal variety, and Hela, who's older and

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.760
more powerful than Thor and Loki both, seems
particularly immune to the whole death thing

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.300
— what with her being the literal goddess
of death and all.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.959
Not only that, but she's at her most powerful
while she's in Asgard, so there's every chance

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.540
that she's still alive and kicking.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.980
We know that Thor's going to need every ally
he can get when Thanos comes to town.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.180
Who better to call for help than his big sister?

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.010
On the other hand, in the comics, Thanos is
in love with Death, and conquers the universe

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.400
to prove his devotion to her.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.480
Considering Hela is the Goddess of Death,
some fans theorize she will return not to

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.600
fight Thanos...but as the object of his affection.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.700
And speaking of Thanos...

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.800
The Mad Titan appears

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.680
During the first post-credits scene, Thor
and Loki talk about the future of the Asgardians,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.200
when suddenly they're interrupted by the arrival
of a gigantic spaceship.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.599
We never find out who, exactly, that ship
belongs to — but it's pretty clear that

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.660
it's Thanos's ride.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.640
"And who is the man in the purple chair?

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.380
He's purple.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:03.600
He has a magic glove.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.880
He doesn't like standing up."

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.100
We've known for years that he'd be the big
bad in the upcoming Avengers: Infinity War.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.900
Considering the cosmic nature of Thor and
the rest of his people, it makes sense that

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.320
they'd be the first to really encounter him
directly.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:17.960
And that's not all.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.840
In July, 2017, Polygon reported on Infinity
War footage shown at D23, the biennial Disney

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:25.780
fan club celebration.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.080
That footage showed a brief moment of Thor
meeting the Guardians of the Galaxy out in

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.600
space.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.500
Considering that Thanos is a known entity
to the Guardians and that Thor's cruising

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.240
around space right now, it seems like a pretty
safe bet that Thanos is going to tangle with

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.700
the God of Thunder before he makes his way
to Earth in Infinity War in summer 2018.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.800
Are you ready for the Space Gem

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.020
You can't have an Infinity War without Infinity
Stones.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.380
We've seen these things floating around the
Marvel Cinematic Universe for years, and Thor:

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.740
Ragnarok was no different.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.720
We got a look at the Time Gem around Doctor
Strange's neck during his brief appearance.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.740
And we saw the Space Gem — also known as
the Tesseract or the Cosmic Cube — sitting

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.280
in the vault on Asgard.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.140
Just before he grabbed Surtur's crown, Loki
stopped and took a closer look at the gem...and

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.900
we never saw just what he did with it.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.620
But, come on...we all know he grabbed that
thing.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:15.120
It's Loki.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.120
That's what he does.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.660
When Infinity War rolls around, Loki will
probably do a double-secret betrayal of his

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.680
brother and offer up the gem to Thanos in
return for, like, a space-duchy or something.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.960
Loki's basically a jerk.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.940
Prepare for him to be jerky with the Space
Gem.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:30.460
Again.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.460
More mirthful Marvel

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.320
The great thing about Marvel movies is that
they can kind of be whatever they want them

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.260
to be, right?

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.940
Winter Soldier was a superheroic spy film.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.020
Ant-Man was a wacky heist movie.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.320
And Spider-Man: Homecoming was basically Home
Alone with web-shooters.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.680
"Oh, hey you guys!"

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.430
But more and more of these movies have leaned
heavily on comedy, and it's a trend that doesn't

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:53.660
show any sign of stopping.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.620
Guardians of the Galaxy, for example, became
a gigantic hit despite the fact that exactly

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:02.990
zero of the characters in it had any name
recognition outside of the deepest corners

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:03.860
of the comic shop.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:08.840
Did you care about Groot or Rocket Raccoon
before the first Guardians of the Galaxy movie?

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.240
Probably not — but you do now.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.980
That's because both of these films, in addition
to all the other wonderful things they do

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.580
with their characters, special effects, and
action set pieces, are also really, really

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:19.100
funny.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:25.240
The same goes for Thor.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.520
If he's your favorite Marvel Comics character,
then you've got some weird taste, dude.

00:05:29.539 --> 00:05:33.590
Thor works great in an ensemble, and Thor
comics have a long history of high adventure

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:34.680
and utter weirdness.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.040
But for most people, he's the just strong
guy with the hammer, and two good but unmemorable

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.100
solo movies didn't do much to change that.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.920
Thor: Ragnarok has flipped the script.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.160
It might even be fair to say that it's put
Thor on the same level as Deadpool when it

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.100
comes to funny superheroes that fans want
to see even more of.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.520
If there's one major explanation we can take
away from the end of this movie, it's that

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.580
Marvel is probably going to double down on
the funny when it comes to adapting its funny-books

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:00.580
to the big screen.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.400
They don't call them "comics" for nothing,
y'know…

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:03.880
Thanks for watching!

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.480
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.060
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

